<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Filarete</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1400–c. 1469</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Antonio di Pietro Averlino</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Filarete, born Antonio di Pietro Averlino, was a Florentine sculptor and architect. He cast the bronze doors of Old St Peter's Basilica in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and from 1451 worked in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francesco-sforza">Francesco Sforza</a>, designing the Ospedale Maggiore and working on the Sforza Castle. His Treatise on Architecture describes Sforzinda, a star-shaped ideal city, one of the first planned ideal cities of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/filarete.jpg" alt="Filarete" width="320" height="365" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Giorgio Vasari · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Delle_vite_de%27_pi%C3%B9_eccellenti_pittori,_scultori,_et_architetti_(1648)_(14799591143).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Antonio di Pietro Averlino was born around 1400 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, where he probably trained as a craftsman. Sources suggest that he worked under Lorenzo Ghiberti, who gave him the name Filarete, meaning a lover of virtue. In the mid 15th century he was expelled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> after being accused of attempting to steal the head of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-the-baptist">John the Baptist</a>, and he moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> and then to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, where he became a ducal engineer and worked on architectural projects for about fifteen years. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giorgio-vasari">Giorgio Vasari</a>, he died in Rome around 1469.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>A commission from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-eugene-iv">Pope Eugene IV</a> led Filarete to cast, over twelve years, the central bronze doors of Old St Peter's Basilica in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, completed in 1445. Although made in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>, the doors show distinct Byzantine influences and seem tied to the medieval era. Some critics have described them as showing a mind of medieval complexity crammed full of exciting but not quite assimilated classical learning.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> he built the Ospedale Maggiore around 1456, planned rationally as a cross within a square with the hospital church at the centre. Surviving parts of the much-rebuilt structure show the Gothic detail of Milan's craft traditions, which sit at odds with his design all'antica, after the Antique. He also worked on the Sforza Castle and on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan-cathedral">Milan Cathedral</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Around 1464 Filarete completed a book of twenty-five volumes that he called his Libro architettonico, usually now called a treatise. It circulated widely in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a>, and its best-known copy, the illustrated Codex Magliabechiano, is dedicated to Piero de' Medici. Written as a fictional narrative, it treats the technical side of architecture and argues against the Gothic style of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, which he calls the barbarous modern style, in favour of classical Roman models.</p><p>Its most best-known part is the plan for Sforzinda, an ideal city named after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francesco-sforza">Francesco Sforza</a>, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, which was never built. Its layout is an eight-point star made by overlaying two squares, inscribed within a circular moat, with towers at the points and gates at the inner angles. Radial avenues led through market squares to a central square, and every other street had a canal. Francesco di Giorgio and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonardo-da-vinci">Leonardo da Vinci</a> are said to have borrowed ideas from him when planning their own ideal cities.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Filarete” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Religious life centred on the Ambrosian rite, unique to the archdiocese, and on great building projects: Gian Galeazzo began Milan Cathedral in 1386 and the Certosa di Pavia in 1396, and Francesco Sforza founded the Ospedale Maggiore, designed by <b>Filarete</b>, in 1456.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-milan">Duchy of Milan · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The central bronze door made by <b>Filarete</b> for the old basilica was enlarged to fit the new narthex, and Maderno's Confessio used marble steps from the old church.</blockquote><a href="/work/st-peters-basilica">St. Peter's Basilica · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.77,11.25&amp;year=1444" aria-label="Florence on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Filarete"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5691 3800h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5691 3800h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.77,11.25&amp;year=1444">What was here in 1444? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
